# What belongs in the source archive (the .tar.gz).
#
# Without this file the archive carried the package and the test
# CODE, but not: the ArcGIS toolbox, the Stata door, the test
# fixtures those tests read, or CITATION.cff. So the tests it
# shipped could not run, and an academic package went out without
# its citation. Long-standing (1.17.3 on PyPI has the same gap);
# fixed in 1.18.0, where it matters more - the toolbox and the
# package became two halves of one thing and belong in one archive.

include CITATION.cff
include MANUAL.md
include MANUAL_BEGINNER.md
include MANUAL_TOPICS.md
include BACKLOG.md

# The doors that pip cannot deliver: a user installs the package,
# then copies these onto the machine by hand.
graft arcgis
# The Pro help SIDECARS. EquiPop.<Tool>.pyt.xml sit next to the
# toolbox and are where Pro reads the comment beside each parameter
# box. They were NEVER SHIPPED - not here, not in the sdist, not in
# any delivery - so a Pro user has only ever had the help they
# happened to generate themselves. BACKLOG 34 has recorded "help
# renders empty in Pro" since v1.16.8 and this is at least part of
# why: the file it reads was not there.
# They are BUILD OUTPUTS, regenerated by arcgis/make_help_xml.py, so
# they are excluded from the repository (BACKLOG 45) and included
# here. Both statements are correct and the distinction is the point:
# do not commit them, do ship them.
include arcgis/*.pyt.xml
graft stata
graft qgis

# Fixtures the shipped tests actually read. (The datasets the Book
# uses moved into equipop/data in 1.19.0 and ride in the wheel.)
graft tests/data

# ...and the helper modules those tests IMPORT. setuptools ships
# files named test*.py by an old default and nothing else beside
# them, so tests/qgis_stub.py - the simulated PyQGIS, present since
# 1.20.0 - had never entered a single archive. Every published
# archive from 1.20.0 to 1.28.0 therefore cannot even COLLECT its
# own test suite: `No module named 'qgis_stub'`. Found in 1.29.0 by
# the check the conventions demand and which had evidently not been
# run since 1.19.0 - unpack the .tar.gz into an empty directory and
# run the whole suite from inside it. Same class of miss as the one
# this file was written for.
include tests/*.py

# THE FIELD PASS. It is not a demo: it is the instrument John and
# Umut run on real data on real machines, and tests/test_field_pass.py
# READS it - so an archive without it cannot pass its own suite. The
# 1.40.5 archive check found exactly that: 11 failures in an unpacked
# .tar.gz, because the file had never been listed here. Same class of
# miss as tests/qgis_stub.py above, and the third time this check has
# earned itself.
include equipop_test_pass.do

# The Bristol County presentation run.
include equipop_demo_bristol.do

# The QGIS install guide travels with the plugin it describes.
include qgis/INSTALL_QGIS_CONTINENTAL.md

# The continental fixture: three clipped WorldPop windows, one lattice.
# Burundi and Rwanda meet at a border here - their bounding boxes overlap
# and not one pixel carries data in both, which is the case that breaks a
# naive extent-based merge. Denmark carries the latitude contrast.
recursive-include tests/fixtures *.tif

# The demo scripts (BACKLOG 107). Listed at last: they sit in the repo
# root, nothing grafted them, and they had never entered an archive.
include demo_*.py
# The RUNNERS at the repository root: run_fetch.py,
# run_raster_folder.py, run_osm_friction.py. FOURTH INSTANCE of the
# miss the three comments above describe, and the worst of them:
# BACKLOG 283 records that run_osm_friction.py is THE ONLY WAY to
# reach the 1.46.0/1.46.1 OSM lattice work, because no door wraps it
# yet. So the sdist carried the headline feature of two releases with
# no way to run it. Found by listing the archive, which is what
# test_packaging now does for every run_*.py at the root.
include run_*.py

# Worked examples, and the Book's sources.
graft examples
graft docs

# Tools that must travel with the code they check. stub_audit.py
# is run in a LIVE QGIS each release (BACKLOG 80) - it is the
# only check that can see the simulator flattering itself, so an
# archive without it ships the problem and not the detector.
# Missed on the first build of 1.29.1 and caught by the archive
# check, which is the second time that check has earned itself.
graft tools

# Never: build leftovers, the Book's compiled output, its node
# packages, or its figures - build.sh regenerates every figure from
# examples/cookbook_0*.py, and they were 4.2 MB of a 4.8 MB archive.
prune docs/book/node_modules
prune docs/book/figs
global-exclude *.docx
global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude __pycache__/*
